Your project targets **.NET 10**, so the best approach is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already wraps `File.*` and `Directory.*` for you.

## Steps

**1. Install the package:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Register in DI (`Program.cs` or `Startup.cs`):**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. In tests — install the testing helpers:**
```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

Then use `MockFileSystem`:
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);
Assert.True(mockFs.Directory.Exists("/data"));
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the full `System.IO` surface — you won't need to extend it every time you use a new file/directory method. The `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem that behaves realistically in tests.

